    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "\"To Whom It May Concern: The white paintings came first; my silent piece came later.\"",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "John Cage: Preface to \"On Robert Rauschenberg, artist, and his work.\"\nIn: Silence. Lectures and Writings by John Cage.\n\nMiddletown, Connecticut: Wesleyan University Press, 1973, p. 98.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "\"[...] do we not already have too much to look at? (Generosity.) Left to myself, I would be perfectly contented with black pictures, providing Rauschenberg had painted them.\n\n(I had one, but unfortunately the new room has a slanting ceiling and besides the wall isn't long enough for it. These are problems that have no solution, such as the suit wearing out.)\"",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "John Cage: \"On Robert Rauschenberg, artist, and his work.\"\n\nIn: Metro. Milano, 1961. Reprinted in: Silence. Lectures and Writings by John Cage.\n\nMiddletown, Connecticut: Wesleyan University Press, 1973, pp. 98-107. [p. 107.]",
      "links": []
    }
